#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# canonical_coordinates

#% text
# `canonical_coordinates([xi, eta], [x, y])` gives canonical coordinates `[r, s]` of the generator `xi d/dx + eta d/dy`: functions with `xi r_x + eta r_y == 0` and `xi s_x + eta s_y == 1`, in which the generator is the translation `d/ds`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `r` is a first integral of `dy/dx == eta/xi` (from `d_solve`), and `s` the integral of `dx/xi` along its solutions; when `xi` or `eta` is zero the coordinates come from one integration.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# The scaling `x d/dx + y d/dy`:

#% cell
canonical_coordinates([x, y], [x, y])
#% output
#= [y/x, log(x)]

#% text
# The rotation:

#% cell
canonical_coordinates([-y, x], [x, y])
#% output
#= [(1/2)*(-x^2 - y^2), atan(y/x)]

#% text
# The projective generator of sl(2):

#% cell
canonical_coordinates([x^2, x*y], [x, y])
#% output
#= [y/x, -1/x]

#% section
# See also

#% text
